==Old Norse==

===Etymology===
Uncertain; either:<ref>{{cite-journal|date=1902 November|journal=Modern Language Notes (MLN)|volume=17|number=7|publisher={{w|The Johns Hopkins University Press}}|doi=10.2307/2917766|jstor=2917766|article=Etymologies|author=George Hempl, {{w|University of Michigan}}|pages=210–212|pageurl=https://www.jstor.org/stable/2917766?seq=1}}.</ref>
* from {{der|non|gem-pro|*kaupōną||to buy}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*kaupô||inkkeeper; merchant}}, from {{der|non|la|caupō||tradesman}}, thus a cognate of {{cog|got|𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍀𐍉𐌽||barter}}; or
* of native origin, related to {{cog|got|𐌺𐌰𐌿𐍀𐌰𐍄𐌾𐌰𐌽||strike, cuff}}, which is {{glossary|morphology|morphologically}} compatible with ''kaupa''.

Senses in other languages derived by relating striking (of hands) to transaction, as with {{m|en|strike a bargain}}, {{m|non|slá kaup}}.

Cognate with {{cog|en|cheap}}, {{cog|de|kaufen}}.

===Verb===
{{non-verb}}

# to [[buy]]

